\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{An integer partition coefficient that cannot normalize}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Take the trivial group, the scalar fiber #{\mathbb Z},
and a cellular circle with one vertex and one labeled loop.
Its transported difference is zero, so
#{\mathcal V=\mathbb Z^2}, #{\extD=0} and, at mass #{2},
#{K=2\bbI}. With weight one, \ref{fdip-00TK} gives
##{Z=4\notin\mathbb Z^\times.}
The [coupled finite coefficient](fdip-00TU) exists over the
integers. Its normalization by #{Z^{-1}} exists only after
a coefficient change in which #{4} is a unit, such as
#{\mathbb Z\to\mathbb Q}. The gauge group order is already
one, so gauge averaging does not remove this obstruction.}
